od Warning issued October 23 at 1:43PM MDT until October 23 at 3:45PM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues.\n\n* WHERE...West Central Montezuma County in southwestern Colorado...\nSoutheastern San Juan County in southeastern Utah along the San\nJuan River from the Four Corners to Lake Powell...\n\n* WHEN...Until 345 PM MDT.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding of rivers, creeks, streams, and other low-lying\nand flood-prone locations is occurring. The San Juan River will\ncontinue to rise this afternoon through Bluff and Mexican Hat.\nProjected peak will occur around midnight tonight.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 143 PM MDT, River forecasts estimate a peak on the San Juan\nRiver near Bluff to exceed 25 feet tonight, with a secondary peak\nexpected overnight Saturday into Sunday. Gauge reports indicated\nhigh accumulations of rainfall since Thursday. Flooding is\nongoing in the warned area.\n- This includes the following streams and drainages...\nTohache Wash, San Juan River, Toh Dahstini Wash and Cowboy Wash.\nFlooding impacts will continue, but no additional rainfall is\nexpected.\n- Some locations that will experience flooding include...\nMexican Hat, Tselakai Dezza, Bluff, Halchita, Montezuma Creek and\nAneth.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-23T15:45:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Montezuma, CO; San Juan, UT","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.9fc5ea75f369e91ca0294c0cdcbc1231828d2450.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.9fc5ea75f369e91ca0294c0cdcbc1231828d2450.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued October 23 at 11:59AM MDT until October 25 at 12:00PM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of southwest Colorado, including the following\nareas, Animas River Basin, Four Corners/Upper Dolores River, San\nJuan River Basin and Southwest San Juan Mountains and Utah,\nincluding the following areas, Arches/Grand Flat,\nCanyonlands/Natural Bridges, La Sal and Abajo Mountains and\nSoutheast Utah.\n\n* WHEN...Through Sunday morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, recent burn scars and other low-lying and\nflood-prone locations. Low-water crossings may be flooded. Storm\ndrains and ditches may become clogged with debris.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Flooding is imminent or ongoing with additional rounds of showers\nand thunderstorms expected through Saturday afternoon. Rivers and\ncreeks may still experience high waters even after rainfall has\nended.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-25T12:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Uncompahgre Plateau/Dallas Divide; Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; Paradox Valley/Lower Dolores River; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Animas River Basin; San Juan River Basin; Southeast Utah; Arches/Grand Flat; La Sal and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.a28b19253bb7550e7a42b701dae47ec9af4c8ee0.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.a28b19253bb7550e7a42b701dae47ec9af4c8ee0.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued October 23 at 7:40AM MDT until October 23 at 6:00PM MDT by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...The eastern San Juan Mountains and Upper Rio Grande Valley.\n\n* WHEN...Until 6 PM MDT this evening.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone locations.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Light to moderate rain showers will continue across the southwest\nmountains through the morning, with scattered showers and\npossible thunderstorms, developing across the region through the\nafternoon. Rain, locally heavy associated with the afternoon\nshowers and thunderstorms, will exacerbate ongoing flooding\nissues across the eastern San Juan mountains and Upper Rio Grande\nvalley.\n- The eastern San Juan Mountains and Upper Rio Grande Valley.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-23T18:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Upper Rio Grande Valley/Eastern San Juan Mountains Below 10000 Ft; Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.f57ec630e3f51d7902083d7b452bfaf33433f7ba.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.f57ec630e3f51d7902083d7b452bfaf33433f7ba.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-10-23T20:04:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KGJT 232004 AFDGJT Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Grand Junction CO 204 PM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Flooding is ongoing across portions of southeast Utah and southwest Colorado, and is expected to continue through Saturday. - Many rivers will experience their crest Friday or Saturday. - Isolated to scattered showers and storms are expected Friday and Sat","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":null,"fwf":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-10-23T06:36:00+00:00","snippet":".DISCUSSION... This is an automatically generated forecast with no discussion. The normal Fire Weather Forecast will resume in its entirety in the spring. ...GUSTY ERRATIC WINDS CAN BE EXPECTED NEAR ANY SHOWERS OR THUNDERSTORMS... ...THE SMOKE DATA IS CALCULATED FROM AN AVERAGE AT 6000 FEET MSL... COZ200-232200- Little Snake Forecast Area- 1235 AM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=FWF&format=CI"},"coagmet":null,"aviation":{"icao":"KTEX","distance_km":30.4,"raw_metar":"METAR KTEX 232115Z AUTO 21007G15KT 10SM SCT048 BKN060 BKN080 10/00 A3018 RMK AO2","temp_f":50,"wind_dir":210,"wind_kt":7,"gust_kt":15,"visibility":"10+","cover":"BKN","flight_category":"VFR